#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093085} :activity/id #uuid "69f4a6c0-8376-56ae-a896-808d467721bf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048695 :topic/id #uuid "b5a55eb9-858d-5acb-ba88-4503d5523513" :topic/math-academy-id 1001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093360 :tutorial/content "Consider the polar curves\n\n$$\nr=2(1+\\cos\\theta)\n$$\n\nand $r=2$. What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/c0/c011bf3b40639e10ec151541d0892d5095b2d983b6ed03247824f06f225c10d9.png)\n\nFirst, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(1 + \\cos θ) &= 2 \\\\\n1 + \\cos θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos θ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for\n\n$$\n\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\nnamely\n\n$$\n\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big(\\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer curve}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner curve}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/7b/7b77af383d0d05d00c91db364e17675dfd45445eb5ac8e2926f8bb90220bc0fe.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2$, and\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta)$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}((2(1 + \\cos θ))^{2} - 2^{2})dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}(4(1 + 2\\cos θ + \\cos^{2} θ) - 4)dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}(2\\cos θ + \\cos^{2} θ)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "87bcd4b8-6965-56b9-8f60-65d778fe217c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1138 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "644bccac-3cde-5911-bd4c-a81e0ad52e7f" :step/math-academy-id 2236 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093139}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092239 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092091 :question/id #uuid "8a885f78-0028-5b6e-80e6-1215f5d934a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf389905-8964-57b6-85bc-2fd738f05f11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092936}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of the Region Bounded by Two Polar Curves With Multiple Intersections"} :step/id #uuid "6fee2e3a-d640-51d8-8bc8-f0525d421f46" :step/math-academy-id 2237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092092 :question/id #uuid "c30d91c2-ee63-5b20-ab39-6d356f711fd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-640"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3361ca6a-66d4-515b-b529-c433a85b7a96" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092935}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of the Region Bounded by Two Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "b48e5386-8b43-5d91-b24a-e05a02d63269" :step/math-academy-id 2241 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093138 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092123 :question/id #uuid "dd29a0e8-2912-531e-a44c-62aa6d604273" :question/math-academy-id "e-7361"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01741ca-0ee3-554c-a2c2-788c97d040fd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050106}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 